    "id": 3,
    "content": "What is the core objective of expansionary fiscal policy during a recession?\n衰退期间扩张性财政政策的核心目标是什么？",
    "options": [
      "Reduce government spending\n减少政府支出",
      "Boost aggregate demand\n提振总需求",
      "Raise tax rates\n提高税率"
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Expansionary fiscal policy uses tax cuts or increased government spending to inject money into the economy, raising aggregate demand to counteract recessionary pressures. For example, the 2009 ARRA ($831B) and 2020 CARES Act ($2.2T) used stimulus during recessions to boost demand when private spending collapsed. This is rooted in Keynesian countercyclical logic—government acts opposite to the cycle to stabilize it.\n扩张性财政政策通过减税或增加政府支出向经济注入资金，提振总需求以对抗衰退压力。例如，2009年ARRA法案（8310亿美元）和2020年CARES法案（2.2万亿美元）在衰退期间刺激经济，当私人支出崩溃时提振需求。这基于凯恩斯主义逆周期逻辑——政府采取与经济周期相反的行动来稳定经济。",
    "category": "1. Macroeconomics / 宏观经济学"
  },
  {
    "id": 4,
    "content": "If the Fed implements contractionary monetary policy (rate hikes), how is the 10-Year Treasury yield likely to respond?\n如果美联储实施紧缩货币政策（加息），10年期国债收益率可能如何反应？",
    "options": [
      "Rise\n上升",
      "Fall\n下降",
      "Remain unchanged\n保持不变"
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "When the Fed raises rates, short-term rates increase, transmitting to longer-term rates through shifts in market expectations and inflation outlooks, pushing the 10-Year Treasury yield higher. In the last four hike cycles, the 10-Year yield increased by an average of 53 basis points. For example, in 2022, yields surged from around 1.5% to over 4% during the 0.25%-to-5.5% hike cycle.\n美联储加息时，短期利率上升，通过市场预期和通胀前景的变化传导到长期利率，推动10年期国债收益率走高。在过去四个加息周期中，10年期收益率平均上升了53个基点。例如，2022年在0.25%-5.5%的加息周期中，收益率从约1.5%飙升至4%以上。",
    "category": "1. Macroeconomics / 宏观经济学"
  },
  {
    "id": 5,
    "content": "Which asset class typically outperforms during peak-to-contraction (recession)?\n在经济周期的顶峰到收缩（衰退）阶段，哪类资产通常表现最好？",
    "options": [
      "Growth stocks\n成长股",
      "Long-term U.S. Treasuries\n长期美国国债",
      "Industrial commodities\n工业大宗商品"
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "During recessions, long-term Treasuries act as safe-haven assets—yields fall sharply as the Fed cuts rates, boosting bond prices. The S&P 500 averages -18% to -22% annual returns during recessions, while the 10-Year Treasury yield falls by 1.4-1.6 percentage points. In 2007-2008, yields dropped over 300 basis points while the S&P 500 fell ~50%.\n衰退期间，长期国债作为避险资产——随着美联储降息，收益率大幅下降，推动债券价格上涨。衰退期间标普500平均年回报率为-18%至-22%，而10年期国债收益率下降1.4-1.6个百分点。2007-2008年，收益率下降超过300个基点，同期标普500下跌约50%。",
    "category": "1. Macroeconomics / 宏观经济学"
  },
  {
    "id": 6,
    "content": "How does a stronger U.S. dollar impact U.S. net exports?\n美元走强如何影响美国净出口？",
    "options": [
      "Increases export competitiveness\n增强出口竞争力",
      "Reduces export competitiveness\n降低出口竞争力",
      "Has no impact\n没有影响"
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "A stronger USD makes U.S. goods more expensive for foreign buyers, reducing export competitiveness and worsening the trade balance. For example, when the USD appreciated 25% from 2014-2016, U.S. manufacturers like Caterpillar and Boeing reported significant headwinds in international sales. Every 10% USD appreciation typically reduces U.S. net exports by 0.5-0.7% of GDP. This is why the Fed monitors currency strength when setting policy—strong dollar acts as automatic monetary tightening.\n美元走强使美国商品对外国买家更贵，降低了出口竞争力并恶化贸易平衡。例如，2014-2016年美元升值25%时，卡特彼勒和波音等美国制造商报告海外销售遭遇重大阻力。美元每升值10%通常会使美国净出口减少GDP的0.5-0.7%。这就是为什么美联储在制定政策时监控货币强度——强美元起到自动货币紧缩作用。",
    "category": "1. Macroeconomics / 宏观经济学"
  },
  {
    "id": 7,
    "content": "If the CCI drops to 75, what does this signal for consumer spending?\n如果消费者信心指数(CCI)降至75，这对消费支出有何信号？",
    "options": [
      "Strong spending intent\n强烈的消费意愿",
      "Caution and weakened spending\n谨慎和消费减弱",
      "No change in spending patterns\n消费模式不变"
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "CCI below 80 indicates consumer caution, weakening discretionary spending on items like dining, travel, and electronics—sectors that drive 68-70% of U.S. GDP. During COVID-19, CCI dropped to a record low of 85.7 in April 2020, corresponding with a 13.6% collapse in consumer spending. Retail stocks like Target and Home Depot are highly sensitive to CCI—when confidence drops below 90, these stocks historically underperform by 5-8%. The baseline CCI reading of 100 was set in 1985.\nCCI低于80表明消费者谨慎，将削弱餐饮、旅游和电子产品等非必需消费支出——这些行业驱动美国GDP的68-70%。在COVID-19期间，CCI在2020年4月降至创纪录低点85.7，对应消费支出暴跌13.6%。塔吉特和家得宝等零售股对CCI高度敏感——当信心指数降至90以下时，这些股票历史上跑输5-8%。CCI基准读数100设定于1985年。",
    "category": "1. Macroeconomics / 宏观经济学"
  },
  {
    "id": 8,
    "content": "When core inflation is slightly lower than expected, which asset most likely goes up?\n核心通胀率略低于市场预期时，哪种资产最可能上涨？",
    "options": [
      "Gold\n黄金",
      "10-year U.S. government bond ETF\n10年期国债ETF",
      "Hang Seng Index\n恒生指数"
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "When core inflation comes in lower than expected, bond prices rise (yields fall) as markets price in potential Fed rate cuts. The 10-year Treasury ETF (IEF) typically rallies 0.5-1.0% on days when core CPI/PCE undershoots by 0.1-0.2 percentage points. For example, when core CPI printed 3.2% vs expected 3.4% in November 2023, TLT (20-year Treasury ETF) surged 2.3% in one session. Gold benefits but less than bonds because lower inflation reduces real yield pressure. Stocks may rise but are less directly sensitive than fixed-income.\n当核心通胀低于预期时，债券价格上涨（收益率下降），因为市场预期美联储可能降息。当核心CPI/PCE低于预期0.1-0.2个百分点时，10年期国债ETF（IEF）通常上涨0.5-1.0%。例如，2023年11月核心CPI为3.2%而预期3.4%时，TLT（20年期国债ETF）单日飙升2.3%。黄金受益但不如债券，因为较低通胀降低了实际收益率压力。股票可能上涨但不如固收资产直接敏感。",
    "category": "1. Macroeconomics / 宏观经济学"
  },
  {
    "id": 9,
    "content": "What is the primary market in stock trading?\n股票交易中的一级市场是什么？",
    "options": [
      "Where investors trade existing stocks among themselves\n投资者之间交易现有股票",
      "Where companies issue new stocks for the first time, like an IPO\n公司首次发行新股的地方，如IPO",
      "Where only preferred stocks are sold\n只出售优先股的地方"
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "The primary market is where companies raise capital by issuing new securities for the first time. Examples include Airbnb's December 2020 IPO (priced at $68, raised $3.5B) and Rivian's November 2021 IPO ($78/share, $12B raised—largest since Alibaba 2014). In the primary market, proceeds go directly to the issuing company. Investment banks like Goldman Sachs and Morgan Stanley act as underwriters, helping price and distribute shares to institutional investors.\n一级市场是公司首次发行新证券以筹集资金的地方。例如Airbnb 2020年12月IPO（定价68美元，筹集35亿美元）和Rivian 2021年11月IPO（78美元/股，筹集120亿美元——自2014年阿里巴巴以来最大规模）。在一级市场，收益直接归发行公司。高盛和摩根士丹利等投资银行作为承销商，帮助定价并向机构投资者分销股票。",
    "category": "2. Stocks / 股票"
  },
  {
    "id": 10,
    "content": "In the secondary market, when you buy Apple stock, who are you buying from?\n在二级市场买苹果股票时，你从谁那里买？",
    "options": [
      "Directly from Apple\n直接从苹果公司",
      "From another investor\n从另一位投资者",
      "From a foreign bank\n从外国银行"
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "The secondary market involves trading existing shares between investors; the company receives no proceeds from these transactions. When you buy Apple stock on NASDAQ, you're buying from another investor—not from Apple itself. The secondary market provides liquidity, allowing investors to enter and exit positions. Daily trading volume on U.S. secondary markets averages $400-600 billion. Brokers like Fidelity, Charles Schwab, and Robinhood facilitate these trades, earning commissions or payment for order flow (PFOF).\n二级市场是投资者之间交易现有股份的地方;公司不从这些交易中获得收益。当你在纳斯达克买苹果股票时,你是从另一位投资者那里买——而不是从苹果公司。二级市场提供流动性,允许投资者进出仓位。美国二级市场日均交易量为4000-6000亿美元。富达、嘉信理财和Robinhood等经纪商促成这些交易,通过佣金或订单流支付(PFOF)赚取收入。",
    "category": "2. Stocks / 股票"
  },
  {
    "id": 11,
    "content": "Which stock type typically gives voting rights?\n哪种股票通常拥有公司决策的投票权？",
    "options": [
      "Preferred shares\n优先股",
      "Common shares\n普通股",
      "ADRs\nADRs"
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Common shares give shareholders voting rights in corporate decisions like board elections and major transactions (e.g., 1 share = 1 vote). Google's dual-class structure illustrates this: Class A shares (GOOGL) have 1 vote, Class C (GOOG) have 0 votes, while founders hold Class B with 10 votes per share. Preferred shares typically have no voting rights but receive fixed dividends and liquidation priority. For example, Warren Buffett's 2008 investment in Goldman Sachs was $5B in preferred shares yielding 10% annually.\n普通股赋予股东在董事会选举和重大交易等公司决策中的投票权（例如1股=1票）。谷歌的双重股权结构说明了这一点：A类股（GOOGL）有1票，C类（GOOG）无投票权，而创始人持有的B类股每股10票。优先股通常无投票权但享有固定股息和清算优先权。例如，巴菲特2008年对高盛的50亿美元投资是优先股，年收益率10%。",
    "category": "2. Stocks / 股票"
  },
  {
    "id": 12,
    "content": "In liquidation, who gets paid before common shareholders?\n公司清算时，谁比普通股股东先获得赔偿？",
    "options": [
      "Common shareholders\n普通股股东",
      "Debt holders and preferred shareholders\n债权人和优先股股东",
      "Only foreign investors\n仅外国投资者"
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "In bankruptcy or liquidation, creditors and preferred shareholders are paid before common shareholders. The priority order is: 1) Secured debt (e.g., bonds backed by assets), 2) Unsecured debt, 3) Preferred stock (gets par value + accumulated dividends), 4) Common stock (residual claims). For example, when Lehman Brothers collapsed in 2008, bondholders recovered ~30-40 cents per dollar, while common shareholders received nothing. This is why preferred shares have lower yields than common dividends—they carry less risk.\n在破产或清算时，债权人和优先股股东在普通股股东之前获得赔偿。优先顺序是：1）有担保债务（如以资产担保的债券），2）无担保债务，3）优先股（获得面值+累积股息），4）普通股（剩余索赔权）。例如，雷曼兄弟2008年倒闭时，债券持有人每美元回收约30-40美分，而普通股股东一无所获。这就是为什么优先股收益率低于普通股股息——风险更小。",
    "category": "2. Stocks / 股票"
  },
  {
    "id": 13,
    "content": "What is ADR?\n什么是ADR？",
    "options": [
      "A type of preferred stock\n一种优先股",
      "A proxy for foreign stocks traded on U.S. exchanges in USD\n在美国交易的外国股票凭证",
      "A stock split mechanism\n一种股票拆分机制"
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "American Depositary Receipts (ADRs) represent shares of foreign companies traded on U.S. exchanges in USD, eliminating currency conversion hassles. Examples: Alibaba (BABA), Taiwan Semiconductor (TSM), and British Petroleum (BP). U.S. banks like JPMorgan deposit the foreign shares abroad and issue ADRs domestically. ADRs trade just like U.S. stocks and provide dividends in USD. There are sponsored ADRs (company-approved, with full disclosure) and unsponsored ADRs (created without company involvement, limited disclosure).\n美国存托凭证（ADR）代表在美国交易所以美元交易的外国公司股份，消除了货币兑换麻烦。例如：阿里巴巴（BABA）、台积电（TSM）和英国石油（BP）。摩根大通等美国银行将外国股票存放在海外并在国内发行ADR。ADR像美国股票一样交易并以美元支付股息。有受托ADR（公司批准，完全披露）和非受托ADR（未经公司参与创建，披露有限）。",
    "category": "2. Stocks / 股票"
  },
  {
    "id": 14,
    "content": "What is a dividend?\n什么是股息？",
    "options": [
      "A company buying back its shares\n公司回购其股份",
      "Payments from profits to shareholders\n从利润中支付给股东的款项",
      "A merger between two companies\n两家公司之间的合并"
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Dividends are cash or stock payments distributed from company earnings to shareholders, typically paid quarterly by mature, profitable companies. For example, Apple pays a quarterly dividend of $0.24/share ($0.96 annually), yielding ~0.5%. High-dividend stocks like AT&T and Verizon yield 5-7%. Dividend-paying companies tend to be stable blue-chips (e.g., Johnson & Johnson has increased dividends for 60+ consecutive years). Growth companies like Tesla and Amazon historically pay no dividends, preferring to reinvest profits.\n股息是从公司盈利中向股东分配的现金或股票支付，通常由成熟盈利公司按季度支付。例如，苹果每季度支付0.24美元/股的股息（年度0.96美元），收益率约0.5%。AT&T和Verizon等高股息股票收益率为5-7%。支付股息的公司往往是稳定的蓝筹股（例如，强生连续60多年增加股息）。特斯拉和亚马逊等成长型公司历史上不支付股息，宁愿再投资利润。",
    "category": "2. Stocks / 股票"
  },
  {
    "id": 15,
    "content": "What is the ex-dividend date?\n什么是除息日？",
    "options": [
      "The date dividends are paid\n支付股息的日期",
      "The cutoff date to qualify for the next dividend\n获得股息资格的截止日期",
      "The announcement date of a stock split\n股票拆分的公告日期"
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "The ex-dividend date is the cutoff date—you must own shares before this date to receive the upcoming dividend. If you buy on or after the ex-dividend date, you won't get the dividend. Stocks typically drop by roughly the dividend amount on the ex-dividend date. Example: If Apple's ex-date is March 10 and it pays $0.24/share on March 15, you must own shares by market close on March 9. The sequence is: Declaration Date → Ex-Dividend Date → Record Date → Payment Date.\n除息日是截止日期——你必须在此日期之前持有股票才能获得即将到来的股息。如果你在除息日或之后买入，你将不会获得股息。股票通常在除息日下跌约等于股息金额。例如：如果苹果的除息日是3月10日，在3月15日支付0.24美元/股，你必须在3月9日收盘前持有股票。顺序是：公告日→除息日→记录日→支付日。",
    "category": "2. Stocks / 股票"
  },
  {
    "id": 16,
    "content": "What happens in a 2-for-1 stock split?\n2拆1的股票拆分中会发生什么？",
    "options": [
      "Shares double, price halves, total value stays the same\n股份翻倍，价格减半，总价值不变",
      "Company pays extra dividends\n公司支付额外股息",
      "Shares are bought back\n股份被回购"
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "In a 2-for-1 stock split, each share is divided into 2, doubling share count while halving price—total value remains unchanged. Example: If you own 100 shares of Tesla at $300/share ($30,000 total), after a 2-for-1 split you own 200 shares at $150/share (still $30,000). Companies split to make shares more affordable for retail investors. Recent examples: Amazon 20-for-1 split (June 2022, from $2,785 to $139.25), Google 20-for-1 split (July 2022), and Nvidia 10-for-1 split (June 2024). Splits are purely cosmetic but often boost liquidity and sentiment.\n在2拆1的股票拆分中，每股被分成2股，股份数量翻倍而价格减半——总价值保持不变。例如：如果你持有100股特斯拉，每股300美元（总计30,000美元），2拆1拆分后你持有200股，每股150美元（仍为30,000美元）。公司拆分股票使零售投资者更容易负担。最近的例子：亚马逊20拆1（2022年6月，从2,785美元降至139.25美元），谷歌20拆1（2022年7月），英伟达10拆1（2024年6月）。拆分纯粹是表面的，但通常会提高流动性和市场情绪。",
    "category": "2. Stocks / 股票"
  },
  {
    "id": 17,
    "content": "What is a stock buyback?\n什么是股票回购？",
    "options": [
      "Issuing new shares\n发行新股",
      "Company repurchasing its own shares to reduce outstanding count\n公司回购自己的股份以减少流通股",
      "Paying dividends in stock\n以股票支付股息"
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Stock buybacks occur when a company repurchases its own shares from the market, reducing outstanding share count and boosting EPS (all else equal). Apple has conducted the largest buyback program in history—over $704 billion since 2013, reducing shares outstanding by ~40%. Buybacks signal management confidence and return cash to shareholders without dividend tax. However, critics argue buybacks inflate EPS artificially and prioritize short-term stock price over long-term investment. In 2022, the U.S. introduced a 1% excise tax on buybacks to encourage dividends.\n股票回购是指公司从市场上回购自己的股票，减少流通股数量并提升EPS（其他条件不变）。苹果进行了史上最大的回购计划——自2013年以来超过7,040亿美元，将流通股减少约40%。回购表明管理层信心并在不征收股息税的情况下向股东返还现金。然而，批评者认为回购人为抬高EPS，优先考虑短期股价而非长期投资。2022年，美国对回购征收1%的消费税以鼓励股息。",
    "category": "2. Stocks / 股票"
  },
  {
    "id": 18,
    "content": "What does EPS measure?\nEPS衡量什么？",
    "options": [
      "Profit per share\n每股利润",
      "Dividend yield\n股息收益率",
      "Market cap\n市值"
