      "FED\nFED",
      "NYSE\nNYSE"
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC), established in 1934 after the Great Depression, oversees all U.S. ETFs to protect investors and maintain fair markets. ETFs must register under the Investment Company Act of 1940 and file Form N-1A (prospectus) disclosing holdings, fees, and risks. The SEC reviews new ETF applications—for example, it took 10 years and multiple rejections before approving Bitcoin Spot ETFs in January 2024 (IBIT, FBTC). The SEC also monitors for market manipulation, front-running, and ensures daily NAV disclosure. The Federal Reserve (FED) controls monetary policy, not securities regulation; NYSE is an exchange, not a regulator.\n美国证券交易委员会（SEC）成立于1934年大萧条后，监管所有美国ETF以保护投资者和维护公平市场。ETF必须根据1940年《投资公司法》注册，并提交N-1A表格（招股说明书），披露持仓、费用和风险。SEC审查新ETF申请——例如，在2024年1月批准比特币现货ETF（IBIT、FBTC）之前，经历了10年和多次拒绝。SEC还监控市场操纵、抢先交易，并确保每日NAV披露。美联储（FED）控制货币政策而非证券监管；纽交所是交易所，不是监管机构。",
    "category": "3. ETF / 交易所交易基金"
  },
