    "correctAnswerIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Money market funds invest in ultra-short-term, high-quality debt instruments with maturities under 13 months, including U.S. Treasury bills (1-12 month maturity, backed by government), commercial paper (unsecured corporate IOUs, typically 30-90 days), certificates of deposit (bank CDs), and repurchase agreements (repos). These funds aim to maintain a stable $1.00 NAV per share while yielding slightly more than savings accounts—currently 4.5-5.3% (as of 2024) vs 0.5% for typical savings. For example, Vanguard Prime Money Market Fund (VMMXX) yields 5.28% with ultra-low risk. However, they're not FDIC-insured, and during the 2008 crisis, Reserve Primary Fund \"broke the buck\" (NAV fell to $0.97) due to Lehman Brothers exposure, triggering industry reforms.\n货币市场基金投资于13个月以下到期的超短期、高质量债务工具，包括美国国库券（1-12个月到期，政府担保）、商业票据（无担保公司借据，通常30-90天）、存款证（银行CD）和回购协议（repos）。这些基金旨在维持稳定的每份额1.00美元NAV，同时收益略高于储蓄账户——目前为4.5-5.3%（截至2024年），而典型储蓄账户为0.5%。例如，先锋优质货币市场基金（VMMXX）收益率5.28%，风险超低。然而，它们没有FDIC保险，2008年危机期间，Reserve Primary Fund因雷曼兄弟敞口"跌破1美元"（NAV降至0.97美元），引发行业改革。",
