    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Private markets—including private equity (PE), venture capital (VC), and hedge funds—are restricted to accredited investors who meet SEC criteria: net worth >$1M (excluding primary residence) OR income >$200K individually ($300K jointly) for the past 2 years. These thresholds aim to protect less-sophisticated investors from illiquid, high-risk investments. For example, investing in Sequoia's VC fund (which backed Google, Apple, Airbnb) requires $250K-$10M minimums and 10-year lock-ups. Private markets offer higher return potential—top-quartile PE funds average 15-20% annual returns vs S&P 500's 10%—but carry liquidity risk, opacity, and concentration. The 2021 JOBS Act expanded access via crowdfunding platforms like AngelList, allowing non-accredited investors to participate with $100-$5K minimums, though liquidity remains limited.\n私募市场——包括私募股权（PE）、风险投资（VC）和对冲基金——仅限符合SEC标准的合格投资者：净资产>100万美元（不包括主要住宅）或过去2年收入>20万美元（个人）或30万美元（联合）。这些门槛旨在保护不够成熟的投资者免受非流动性、高风险投资的影响。例如，投资红杉的风投基金（支持过谷歌、苹果、Airbnb）需要25万-1000万美元的最低投资额和10年锁定期。私募市场提供更高回报潜力——顶级四分位PE基金平均年回报15-20%，而标普500为10%——但具有流动性风险、不透明性和集中度。2021年JOBS法案通过AngelList等众筹平台扩大准入，允许非合格投资者以100-5000美元的最低投资额参与，但流动性仍然有限。",
